        {
          "fact_id": "prose-CP-4-8.r0.c0",
          "label": "Default detail, suspension and funded exit",
          "table_id": "prose-CP-4-8",
          "row_id": "r0",
          "field": "c0",
          "amount": null,
          "text": "Payment/nonpayment and notice conditions must not be conflated. Interest gets 30 days (§6.01(a)); principal and due tender payments do not receive that same grace (§6.01(b)). Other covenants require notice from trustee or 25% holders and 60 days, reporting 180 days; §5.01 requires notice but not that passage of time (§6.01(c)). Cross-debt default is a final-maturity payment failure or acceleration, not every debt covenant breach; threshold is at least USD50 million and excludes Securitization Entities in that limb. Final/nonappealable judgments use in excess of USD50 million and a 60-day unpaid/unstayed test. Collateral failure above USD50 million has a 60-day persistence limb, while a court pleading repudiating validity is separately covered (§6.01(h)). The Company or Significant Subsidiary insolvency events are covered, but automatic acceleration under the controlling §6.02 applies to Company bankruptcy events; others use the stated declaration mechanism. Majority can rescind acceleration under conditions and waive nonpayment-excepted past defaults; it cannot simply waive continuing ordinary payment defaults for all dissenters (§§6.02,6.04,6.07).",
