        {
          "locator": "line 53",
          "text": "| B02 Product mix | Documentary Fact | Power & Control: actuators, ignition/engine technology, pumps/valves, motors, batteries, sensors, hoists and microwave electronics. Airframe: latches, rods/seals, security/displays, restraints, interiors, lighting, parachutes and testing. Non-aviation: ground restraints, space actuators, gas-turbine controls and heavy-equipment refueling. S1 p.5: “Products are sold in the original equipment and aftermarket market channels.” CP-1 SG04/SG08/SG09 retain segment sales below. | Diverse component families reduce single-product dependency, but segment mix and end-market cycles still affect earnings → preserve all three segments and do not equate diversification with immunity to aircraft-sector shocks. Individual product revenue/margin shares: Not disclosed. |",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 54",
          "text": "| B03 Revenue-channel estimate | Management Language | S1 p.4: “We estimate that approximately 55% of our net sales in fiscal year 2024 were generated from the aftermarket”; same page estimates approximately 90% proprietary products. These are issuer estimates, not a new calculation. | Proprietary installed parts and replacement demand can support repeat revenue and pricing discretion → potentially more durable cash generation, conditional on utilization, certification and competition; not subscription/contracted recurring revenue. Defense includes OEM and aftermarket, so the Note 15 commercial aftermarket rows are not the entire aftermarket estimate. |",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 55",
          "text": "| B04 Aftermarket margin/stability characterization | Management Language | S1 p.4: “Historically, these aftermarket revenues have produced a higher gross profit and have been more stable than net sales to original equipment manufacturers”; aircraft life estimated approximately 25–30 years and product life cycle in excess of 50 years. S1 p.9 explicitly says commercial aftermarket historically produced higher gross profit margins than commercial OEM. | Long platform lives can spread replacement consumption across cycles; channel-mix shifts still move margins → historical resilience is a business mitigant, not a guaranteed margin or forward cash-flow floor. Aftermarket standalone gross margin: Not disclosed; no invented margin spread. |",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 56",
          "text": "| B05 End markets / concentration | Management Language | S1 p.7 estimates FY2024 commercial/regional/business/general aviation aftermarket approximately 31%, commercial aerospace OEM approximately 27%, defense approximately 40%, non-aerospace approximately 2%; “Our top ten customers ... approximately 42%”; “None ... greater than 10%.” Note 3 p.121 separately states no customer individually accounted for 10% or more in any supplied annual year. | Customer breadth tempers individual counterparty dependence, but common aerospace/defense drivers can correlate demand → monitor sector/platform and distributor exposures beyond single-customer thresholds. Approximate narratives are not exact segment-account reconciliations; boundary wording at the threshold is preserved. |",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 57",
          "text": "| B06 Geography | Documentary Fact | S1 p.143: “Net sales are measured based on the geographic destination of sales.” FY2024/FY2023/FY2022 United States sales 5,032/4,265/3,496; foreign 2,908/2,320/1,933 (USD millions). S1 p.121: foreign customers “primarily in Western Europe, Canada and Asia.” | Destination diversity broadens demand sources but adds FX, export and cross-border operating exposures → do not infer functional-currency exposure, unrestricted foreign cash or legal asset location from customer geography. Individual-country detail: Not disclosed as material. |",
