          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 60",
          "text": "### Business, ownership and operating economics",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 62",
          "text": "**Structure and control.** TD Group is the listed holding company; TransDigm Inc. is its wholly-owned operating subsidiary and operating debt issuer. TD Group and other subsidiaries have guarantor roles in the reported 2033 financing. Power & Control, Airframe and Non-aviation are reporting segments, not legal obligors. Consequently, consolidated earnings, reported cash and segment assets do not identify cash available at a particular issuer or collateral available to a particular creditor. Basis: CP-1 T4.2 and CP-1A B01/B02/O01. Beneficial ownership, voting control, board independence, affiliate arrangements and sponsor identity/support are Not supplied in the necessary detail; no controlling sponsor or support willingness is inferred from a public listing (O02–O04, GT04/GT09).",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 64",
          "text": "**Revenue durability and competition.** Management estimates approximately 55% of FY2024 sales came from aftermarket and approximately 90% from proprietary products; it characterizes aftermarket revenues as historically higher gross profit and more stable than OEM sales. These remain **Management Language**, not independently established competitive statistics. Long aircraft/product lives and qualification investments can slow switching and sustain replacement demand, creating pricing discretion and earnings visibility. However, a proprietary design is not automatically sole-source, and installed-base opportunity is not backlog. Predominantly point-in-time recognition makes shipments and control transfer relevant to realized revenue. Basis: CP-1A B03/B04/B08/B09 and CT01; inherited S1 pp.4, 7, 9, 120–121.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 66",
          "text": "**Price, volume and mix.** Value-based pricing is management's stated approach, not a quantified FY2024 price contribution. Utilization, fleet age/size, airline economics, OEM production schedules and defense budgets are supported volume mechanisms (B09/B10). Pricing can offset cost pressure only if customers accept increases and regulatory/contract conditions permit them. OEM/aftermarket and defense mix can change consolidated margins even when total sales rise. The observed annual sales increase must not be labeled wholly organic, price-driven or volume-driven: acquired contributions and a checked price/volume bridge are Not supplied (CP-1A X01/X02/G07).",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 68",
          "text": "**Diversification with correlated exposures.** Multiple component families, direct customers, distributors and destination markets broaden demand sources (B02/B05–B07). Note-level customer disclosure supports the absence of an individually dominant customer at the stated reporting threshold; management also reports meaningful top-customer concentration. Such breadth reduces isolated counterparty risk, but commercial aerospace utilization, OEM production and government spending remain common drivers. Distributors can shift stocking ahead of end-user consumption. Destination sales do not reveal functional-currency exposure, cash location or accessibility. This supports diversification as a mitigant, not immunity from sector stress.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 70",
          "text": "**Costs and capex.** Product teams, decentralized manufacturing and investment in tooling/automation provide potential productivity levers (B11/B12). Conversely, qualified supply chains, specialized labor and certification constraints impede rapid substitutions and can generate expediting, idle-capacity or inventory costs. Historical signed capex is modest relative to operating cash, but the maintenance/growth split and fixed/variable cost structure are Not supplied. Therefore, capex flexibility is bounded: reducing investment cannot be assumed costless to reliability, certification or future competitiveness. Acquisitions are separate cash uses, not capex within CP-1 K02.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 72",
          "text": "**Cash conversion.** CP-1 CF22, K03 and K12 show stronger operating cash generation and a smaller annual cash use in the complete changes-in-assets/liabilities block in FY2024. Inventory, receivables and unbilled performance can still absorb cash before collection; advance receipts provide partial offsets (CP-1A B13/CT02). Interest, taxes, integration costs, acquisition spend and distributions prevent adjusted EBITDA or annual CFO less capex from representing freely distributable cash. CP-1's ordinary EBITDA and EBITDA As Defined are different reported non-GAAP measures; neither is automatically accepted economic earnings or legal EBITDA.",
