        {
          "locator": "line 136",
          "text": "### Ranked strengths, weaknesses and key risks",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 138",
          "text": "**Strengths**",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 140",
          "text": "1. **Installed-base replacement economics — Analyst Interpretation.** CP-1A B03/B04/B09 supports a conditional durability thesis: long-lived qualified components and proprietary-content estimates create repeat replacement demand and potential pricing discretion, which can stabilize debt-service earnings relative to reliance solely on new aircraft production. Credible counterevidence is utilization sensitivity, government remedies and supply constraints (B10/B11/B14). The view would strengthen with verified platform/aftermarket profitability and stable end-user orders; it would weaken with sustained substitution, qualification loss, adverse pricing acceptance or utilization-driven demand erosion.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 142",
          "text": "2. **Improving historical earnings and cash conversion — Analyst Interpretation.** CP-1 IS06, K04–K06 and K12 show stronger annual profitability and conversion, while CF22/CF23 supports positive CFO less capex. Greater operating cash generation improves the ability to meet cash obligations before capital allocation. Counterevidence is continuing working-capital cash use, acquisition/integration demands and untested add-backs (K03, CF24, EB06–EB09; CP-1A CT02). Consistent cash collections, evidenced maintenance needs and a challenged earnings-to-cash bridge would strengthen this view; margin-to-cash divergence would weaken it.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 144",
          "text": "3. **Breadth and documented historical financing execution — Analyst Interpretation.** CP-1A B02/B05–B07 and X06/BF02 support product/customer breadth and completed tenor extensions. These reduce isolated operating exposures and selected historical funding pressure. Common sector drivers and secured substitution remain counterevidence, while missing market inputs prevent extrapolating access. Better exposure detail and verified refinancing terms would improve confidence; concentrated order deterioration or materially more restrictive financing would reduce it.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 146",
          "text": "**Weaknesses**",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 148",
          "text": "1. **Debt-funded extraction limits retained creditor cushion — Analyst Interpretation; Material risk.** CP-1A BF01/GT01/GT02 and CP-1 BS14 establish borrowing linked to a non-earning equity payout and a substantial accrued payment. The mechanism leaves debt service behind while removing cash, restricting the inference from gross base-date liquidity and elevating future capital-allocation monitoring. No FY2024/FY2023 buybacks, explicit reporting and historical extensions are credible contrary evidence, not reversal of the dividend. Sustained retention, restrained distributions and evidenced debt reduction would improve the view; further debt-funded shareholder returns would weaken it.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 150",
          "text": "2. **Leverage leaves less tolerance for earnings or financing setbacks — Analyst Interpretation; Material risk.** CP-1 K07/K09 shows carrying debt and carrying leverage increased despite stronger earnings. In the downside chain, lower earnings or new borrowing would increase the burden on retained cash and refinancing. Improved operating-income/net-interest coverage (K11) is counterevidence, but is neither cash coverage nor covenant headroom. Accepted earnings, sustained cash retention and verified debt retirement would improve the view; persistent cash-funded shortfalls or incremental debt without contribution would weaken it.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 152",
          "text": "3. **Acquisitions create competing cash demands and integration exposure — Analyst Interpretation; Material risk.** CP-1A X01/X02/GT03 and CP-1 CF24 show repeated acquisitions, including debt-linked CPI funding. New businesses may add earnings and diversification, but cash integration, preliminary allocations and uncertain contribution can postpone creditor benefit. Multiple added product exposures are genuine counterevidence to a purely adverse acquisition view. Demonstrated acquired cash returns and successful controls integration would improve it; acquisition-driven debt growth without realized contribution would weaken it.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 154",
          "text": "4. **Sector and qualified-supply sensitivity can transmit into cash quickly — Analyst Interpretation; Material risk.** CP-1A B07/B10/B11/B14 supports utilization, OEM, budget, distributor and certification dependencies. Correlated volume declines or production constraints can reduce shipments, pressure mix and absorb inventory cash, weakening liquidity before management can fully adjust costs. Aftermarket content and customer/product breadth mitigate this mechanism. Stable end-use demand, qualified supply and collections would support the view; persistent order cancellations, inventory accumulation, labor disruption or contract remedies would weaken it. No current adverse event is inferred.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
        {
          "locator": "line 156",
          "text": "The ranking reflects supported mechanics, not numerical risk probabilities. Missing liquidity, market, ownership and legal inputs are separately recorded as gaps, not additional adverse behavior. CP-1A's bounded governance rubric is not reproduced as a CP-2 composite rating or independently rescored.",
          "formula": null,
          "cached_value": null,
          "hidden": false,
          "data_type": null,
          "number_format": null,
          "note": null
        },
