        },
        {
          "locator": "line 121",
          "text": "**Operational → revenue → margin → cash → liquidity → leverage/refinancing downside chain.** A utilization slowdown, OEM disruption or qualified-supplier interruption (CP-1A B10/B11/B14) reduces spares demand and/or deliverable components → shipment revenue falls or shifts away from higher-margin aftermarket (B04/B08) → specialized production costs, labor constraints and input inflation are not fully offset by pricing, compressing margin (B09/B11) → lower earnings and slower collections or excess inventory weaken operating cash conversion (B13; CP-1 K03/K12) → debt service and committed cash uses consume accessible liquidity faster than planned (CP-1 IS07/BS14; accessibility remains unproven) → weaker earnings with retained or additional debt raises leverage and makes refinancing more dependent on market willingness, eligible collateral and legal permissions (K09/F-register; CT04/CT05). Management can address pricing, sourcing, schedules, inventory, collections, optional capex sequencing and discretionary capital allocation, but their size, speed and effectiveness require the missing forecast/cost/access inputs. No numerical downside or covenant breach is asserted.",
